course: french
lesson: 19
title: At the café: ordering in French
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A French café visit has a little script: bonjour when you sit down, je voudrais... s'il vous plaît to order, merci when you are served, l'addition, s'il vous plaît when you want to pay, and au revoir, bonne journée as you leave.
explain: Never click your fingers or shout "Garçon!" as in old films: today that is rude; catch the waiter's eye, smile and say S'il vous plaît, monsieur ! or Excusez-moi !
explain: Some orders surprise English speakers: un café is a tiny strong black coffee, une limonade is fizzy, and Quel parfum ? asks which flavour, not which perfume.
explain: Picture Riley in a Paris café waiting ages for the bill: the waiter isn't rude, he is being polite by not rushing you, and he brings the bill only when you ask.
explain: Even if the waiter speaks English, trying your French first is polite, and most waiters will happily help you.
fact: To call a waiter politely say Excusez-moi, monsieur ! or S'il vous plaît ! with a small raised hand; shouting garçon (boy) is old-fashioned and rude today.
fact: In France un café is a small strong black coffee (an espresso); children usually order un chocolat chaud, un jus d'orange or une limonade.
fact: un croque-monsieur is a toasted ham and cheese sandwich; un croque-madame is the same with a fried egg on top; croquer means to crunch.
fact: une limonade is fizzy lemonade, like Sprite; un citron pressé is fresh squeezed lemon with water and sugar.
fact: un diabolo is fizzy lemonade with a syrup: diabolo menthe is green (mint), diabolo grenadine is red and diabolo fraise is strawberry.
fact: L'addition, s'il vous plaît ! means "The bill, please!"; French waiters usually wait until you ask for it.
fact: de l'eau plate is still water and de l'eau gazeuse is sparkling water; une carafe d'eau is tap water and is free in French restaurants.
fact: A French hot-dog is often a sausage poked into a baguette; the h of hot-dog is not said.
fact: des frites et un Coca means chips and a cola; starting with bonjour and trying French is always welcome.
fact: un jambon-beurre is a baguette with ham and butter, one of the most popular sandwiches in France.
fact: Say merci when you are served, and au revoir and bonne journée (have a good day) when you leave, not before.
fact: une glace is an ice cream; Quel parfum ? means "Which flavour?"; parfum means both perfume and flavour; common flavours are vanille, chocolat, fraise and citron.
fact: Slang here: "mortified" means terribly embarrassed; "thimble" is a tiny cup for sewing; "proper job" (West Country) means great; "pop" (Ireland, North) means fizzy drink; "wicked" (Boston) means brilliant; "heaps" (Australia) means very; "this slaps" (US Gen Z) means this is great; "pal" and "aye" (Scotland) mean mate and yes; "eh" (Canada) asks you to agree.
exercise: How should Jayden call the waiter in Paris? | Excusez-moi, monsieur ! | Garçon ! with a click of the fingers | Hé, toi, le serveur ! | Oi, monsieur, viens ici !
exercise: Ella orders un café in Lyon. What does she get? | a tiny cup of strong black coffee | a big mug of milky coffee | a café table by the window | a glass of iced coffee with cream
exercise: Which is a typical drink for a child to order? | un chocolat chaud | un café noir | un grand café crème | un expresso double
exercise: What is un croque-monsieur? | a toasted ham and cheese sandwich | a crunchy biscuit for gentlemen | a man who crunches food | a toasted sandwich with an egg on top
exercise: What does un croque-madame have that un croque-monsieur doesn't? | a fried egg on top | a lady's hat on top | extra cheese inside | jam instead of ham
exercise: Lily orders une limonade. What does she get? | fizzy lemonade | fresh lemon juice | a lemon cake | a slice of lemon
exercise: How do you ask for fresh squeezed lemon? | un citron pressé | une limonade fraîche | un jus de limonade | un citron gazeux
exercise: What colour is un diabolo menthe? | green | red | blue | yellow
exercise: How do you ask for the bill? | L'addition, s'il vous plaît ! | La facture, maintenant ! | Le prix, s'il te plaît ! | Combien d'argent, monsieur ?
exercise: The waiter asks "Plate ou gazeuse ?" about water. What is plate? | still | on a plate | sparkling | hot
exercise: How do you ask for free tap water? | Une carafe d'eau, s'il vous plaît. | Une bouteille d'eau gazeuse, s'il vous plaît. | De l'eau plate en bouteille, merci. | Un verre de limonade, s'il vous plaît.
exercise: How is the h of hot-dog said in France? | It is not said | It is breathed out strongly | It is said like "k" | It is said like "sh"
exercise: Jack says "chips and a Coke, pal". What is the French? | des frites et un Coca | des chips et un Coca | des frites et un café | des pommes et un Coca
exercise: What is in un jambon-beurre? | ham and butter in a baguette | jam and butter on toast | ham, mayonnaise and salad | cheese and butter in a roll
exercise: When should you say au revoir at a café? | When you leave | When the drinks arrive | When you sit down | When you order
exercise: What does bonne journée mean? | have a good day | good night | good morning | good journey home
exercise: The ice cream man asks "Quel parfum ?" What does he want to know? | Which flavour you want | Which perfume you wear | How many scoops you want | Whether you want a cone
test: Jayden's mum is mortified ("mortified" means terribly embarrassed) when he shouts "Garçon !" Why? | Calling a waiter garçon is old-fashioned and rude today | Garçon is a bad swear word in French | The waiter was a girl, so it should be garçonne | Waiters in Paris only answer in English
test: Theo says croque-monsieur is a "proper job name". ("Proper job" means great.) What would happen if he ordered a croque-madame? | It would come with a fried egg on top | It would come without any ham, just cheese and bread | It would be served by a lady | It would come with chocolate
test: Lily says, "Sure I wanted lemon juice, not pop." ("Pop" means fizzy drink.) What should she order next time? | un citron pressé | une limonade | un diabolo menthe | un Coca
test: Mason shouts "Wicked!" when his diabolo menthe arrives bright green. What is it? | lemonade with mint syrup | a devil-shaped cake with green icing on top | a cartoon character | green tea with milk
test: Riley says, "Do they forget us? Heaps slow." ("Heaps" means very.) What's the truth? | Waiters wait until you ask for l'addition | French waiters are always very rude to tourists from abroad | The café has closed but nobody told them | The bill is always free for children
test: Harry asks, "Plate? Like a dinner plate?" What does eau plate mean? | still water | water on a plate | sparkling water | hot water
test: Ava says her French hot-dog "slaps". ("This slaps" means this is great.) How is it different? | The sausage is often poked into a baguette | It has no sausage at all, just melted cheese and onions | It is made of fish | It is served cold with jam
test: Jack says "Aye, that" to "Des frites et un Coca ?" ("Aye" means yes.) What would have been more polite? | Start with bonjour and try French first | Speak louder and slower in English until he understands | Point at the menu and say nothing | Ask for the manager straight away
test: Mia asks, "Same thing, eh?" about un jambon-beurre. ("Eh" asks you to agree.) What's different from many sandwiches? | It is just ham and butter in a baguette, no mayo or salad | It has jam instead of ham | It is made with sliced white bread | It is a hot drink
test: Ben's friend jokes, "Mate, you're leaving already?" when Ben says "Merci, au revoir !" as the drinks come. What should Ben say then? | Just merci, and au revoir later when leaving | Bonne nuit, and nothing else | Bonjour, again and again | L'addition, before drinking
test: Ruby whispers, "Perfume?! Do I smell?" when the man asks "Quel parfum ?" How should she answer? | Chocolat, s'il vous plaît ! | Non, merci, je ne porte pas de parfum ! | Je voudrais un parfum. | Merci, au revoir !
test: ★★★ A diabolo costs 3 euros, a croque-monsieur 6 euros and a carafe d'eau is free. Two friends share one croque-monsieur and have one diabolo each, plus a carafe. What is l'addition? | 12 euros | 9 euros | 15 euros | 13 euros
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate manner of summoning a waiter in France? | Eye contact accompanied by the words excusez-moi or s'il vous plaît | The clicking of the fingers accompanied by the word garçon | A loud whistle followed by the word monsieur | Remaining silent until the waiter approaches of his own accord
exam: Identify the beverage served when un café is requested in France. | A small strong black coffee | A large milky coffee | A glass of hot chocolate | A cup of tea with lemon
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning une carafe d'eau. | It is tap water, customarily provided free of charge | It is sparkling water, charged at a premium | It is bottled still water imported from abroad | It is lemonade served in a jug
exam: It is given that an ice-cream vendor enquires "Quel parfum ?" Determine the nature of the information requested. | The flavour desired | The fragrance worn by the customer | The quantity of scoops desired | The method of payment
